 "title": "Idle baseline, the hart-matched triple, and the power ladder",
 "predicted": {
  "what": "Board power linear in harts at 40.6 mW/hart (1.3 W per shire at per-shire 32): 5.2 / 10.4 / 15.6 / 20.8 / 41.6 W above a 23-25 W idle. The hart-matched triple at 128 harts (B4C 4x32, W8B_16 8x16, INT16_8 16x8) agrees within hp's own +/-0.5 W equivalence band, i.e. power follows harts and not geometry. Every board-power statistic here is the MEDIAN of the 10 Hz samples in the last 4.0 s of a launch, after device open, kernel load and the power ramp, and the within-arm scatter is reported beside the between-arm difference so the +/-0.5 W test has a stated power. Field drift 5-8 mK/s and at least one FFC in the 180 s pre-idle.",
  "mk": null,
  "direction": "no spatial claim; this block is the control for every later one",
  "basis": "launch.md section 5 (the per-shire-count sweep is unmeasured on aifoundry2 and this settles it); hp/README.md's +/-0.5 W band, measured -0.21 W for INT16@32 vs PER16@32 at ~14 W",
  "anchor": null
 },
 "pass_rule": "PASS if board power is linear in harts with the three 128-hart legs inside +/-0.5 W of each other (medians of the last 4.0 s of each launch) and mhz.minion reads 800 in every rung. SESSION GATE, with its window and its reference named: on the frame mean over the UNI32 rung PLUS the 30 s IDLE phase that follows it -- the peak of an 8.5 s step lands in that idle, because the registered arrival of a step on the back is 4-14 s -- measured against the mean of the 180 s pre-session idle, the rise must be at least 300 mK. Below that the camera is not seeing the card and the session stops here. (Version 1 asked for 'of order 500 mK' with no window and no reference: against the pre-ladder baseline the accumulated rise is about 1.7 K and passes easily, against the preceding INT16 rung the UNI32 increment is about 400 mK and fails -- the most expensive ambiguity in the file.) FRAME-EXTENT CHECK, from the same UNI32 monopole image and recorded before E62-ORBIT commits 12.9 min: the die footprint's pixel extent, the clipped-pixel fraction, and whether any regulator hot spot is in frame, each compared against PROTOCOL.md section 3's table. Everything in section 3 -- the dwell split, the clipping rule, the nominal 0.156 mm/px and section 9 item 8's claim that the regulators are out of frame -- rests on a lens distance PROTOCOL.md itself calls planned, and the camera is live and read-only, so this costs nothing.",
